import "core"


# A class's name may be a prefix of its members' names.
  A note's checkbox is read as one of two things, and the two are named under the name of the class
  they are both members of, so that what a checkbox is read as is one name with two readings under
  it rather than two unrelated names. That makes the class name a strict prefix of every member
  name, which nothing else in the checked standard library does — and a relative name is matched by
  its suffix, so a member named under its own class is exactly the case where the two could be taken
  for each other.
  Written in both of the forms a class is written in: [/Item], joined to member by member as a note
  joins its readings to [/Catln/Doc/DShow], and [/Thing], which lists its members and so can carry a
  method every member answers.
  [/Thing] lists its members with their fields written out, which is the only way a class carrying a
  method may list them — a class listing members declared apart from it does not seal over their
  fields, and the method is then never covered. See
  @test/Integration/disabled/classMemberByName.ct@.
class(Item)

data(Item/Simple(x -> Integer))

every(Item/Simple.., isa= [/Item])

data(Item/Fancy(x -> Integer, y -> Integer))

every(Item/Fancy.., isa= [/Item])

# The values a page of such members declares, written out and never defined — the shape a note's
  page holds a checkbox as.
/Item/Simple(x= 1)

/Item/Fancy(x= 1, y= 2)

class(Thing, [Thing/Simple(x -> Integer), Thing/Fancy(x -> Integer, y -> Integer)])

:/Thing.total -> Integer
  # What a [/Thing] comes to, which each member answers for itself. Declared on the class, so the
    two definitions below are two arms of one method rather than two methods sharing a name — and
    the class listing its members is what makes the two of them cover it.
:/Thing/Simple(x).total= x
:/Thing/Fancy(x, y).total= x + y

main{io -> IO} =
  #assert(test= /Thing/Simple(x= 3).total == 3)
  #assert(test= /Thing/Fancy(x= 3, y= 4).total == 7)
  io
